# The first iyi program.
#
#   ./bin/crystal run samples/iyi/hello.iyi
#
# Everything below compiles and runs today. What is still missing is listed at
# the bottom — this file is meant to stay honest about how far the
# implementation actually reaches.

# R-1: the compilation-unit header. A module is the unit of compilation and of
# recompilation; `import` edges between modules form a DAG. Reading a module's
# `.iyimod` is what will one day replace re-analysing its source.
module samples/hello

# R-3: a trait is a named set of requirements. `abstract def` marks a method an
# implementer must provide. Traits replace Crystal's open classes: a type's
# method set is knowable without reading the whole program.
pub trait Greet
  abstract def greet : String
end

pub trait Loud
  abstract def shout : String
end

# R-2: `pub` marks a declaration as exported, and exported declarations carry
# full signatures. That signature is the module's contract, and the only thing
# a consumer ever needs to read.
pub struct User
  getter name : String

  def initialize(@name : String)
  end
end

pub struct Robot
  getter id : Int32

  def initialize(@id : Int32)
  end
end

# `impl Trait for Type` — the replacement for reopening a class. R-3's orphan
# rule requires it to live in the module defining either the trait or the
# type, and that is enforced: both are defined right here. `modules.iyi` shows
# the cross-module case, where the choice actually costs something.
impl Greet for User
  def greet : String
    "Hello, #{name}!"
  end
end

# A type may implement several traits...
impl Loud for User
  def shout : String
    greet.upcase
  end
end

# ...and a trait may be implemented by several types.
impl Greet for Robot
  def greet : String
    "BEEP #{id}"
  end
end

# `impl` is the only way to give a type a trait. SPEC.md II.8 — each of these
# is refused, and each for its own reason:
#
#   struct User
#     include Greet     # a type acquires a trait by having an impl, whose
#   end                 # location R-3 can check; `include` has no such rule
#
#   import samples/hello/greet::*   # a trait exports no names to bring
#                                   # into scope — a trait method is resolved
#                                   # from the receiver, never from an
#                                   # import's names
#
#   impl Greet for Loud         # a blanket impl in disguise
#
# And leaving a requirement unmet is an error where the impl is written, not
# where the type is later used:
#
#   impl Loud for Robot
#   end
#   # => impl Loud for Robot is missing a method required by the trait: shout

# Not exported. No signature required, because nothing outside this module can
# depend on it — inference inside a module costs only what the module costs.
def describe(item)
  item.greet
end

# A trait bound on a method's own type parameter — SPEC.md II.7 rule 3. The
# method exists for every `T`; the bound is checked where `T` binds, so a call
# with a type that does not implement `Greet` is rejected at that call:
#
#   announce(42)
#   # => Int32 does not implement Samples::Hello::Greet, required by `T`
#   #    in `announce`
#
# This is the shape Kemal's router needs: `&block : Context -> B forall
# B : IntoBody` replaces a per-request runtime type check with one at compile
# time. Note this is *not* the impl-level bound below — nothing here is
# conditional.
pub def announce(item : T) : String forall T : Greet
  "-> #{item.greet}"
end

# Ruby ergonomics, unchanged and free to compile: blocks, string
# interpolation, everything-is-an-expression, local inference.
puts User.new("iyi").greet
puts User.new("iyi").shout
puts Robot.new(42).greet

# A trait as a type: both implementers dispatch through it.
crowd = [User.new("crystal"), Robot.new(7)] of Greet
crowd.each { |member| puts describe(member) }

puts announce(Robot.new(9))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not yet implemented
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * Trait bounds on a generic *impl*'s parameters (`impl Show for Box(T)
#   forall T : Show`) parse but are rejected — unlike the bound on `announce`
#   above, which works. A bound on an impl makes the impl conditional:
#   `Box(Int32)` would implement `Show` only if `Int32` did, which has to be
#   checked where the type is instantiated rather than where the impl is
#   written. A different mechanism, and the harder of the two.
#
# * Dictionary-passing generics is unimplemented: `announce` above is
#   monomorphised the way Crystal monomorphises it.
#
# * `.iyimod` reaches the front end and stops there. `modules.iyi` and
#   `immutable.iyi` compile with the imported module's source deleted, but a
#   build that reads artifacts cannot yet produce a program, because the
#   artifact carries declarations and no object code (SPEC.md IV.1f).
#
# * The prelude under this file is iyi's own, 833 lines of it, and that is why
#   building it costs 0.32 s rather than 2.10 s. It is not a standard library
#   and does not pretend to be one: no `Float64#to_s`, no `Range`, no `Set`,
#   no formatting, and `raise` is a panic that prints and exits. What is in it
#   is what a sample called for (SPEC.md, "0.1.0", item 3).
#
# See `modules.iyi` for `import` and its `::*` and `::{...}` forms across
# files, `generics.iyi` for implementing a trait for a generic type and for
# associated types, bounds and requirements, and `collections.iyi` for
# `Enumerable` ported onto all of it.
